  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Subtropical Storm Josephine, continues to accelerate\nnortheastward away from our area. As expected a marked decrease\nin winds have occurred with seas still running quite high off\nour coast, but lower than previous days. With continued large \nsurf and longer period swell, coastal flooding impacts along \nvulnerable locations of the coast will continue through early\ntonight, especially along Hatteras Island where dune structure \nis fragile. The National Parks Service and NCDOT have reported \nsubstantial beach erosion from south Nags Head to Buxton, along \nwith significant overwash occurring on side streets in Rodanthe.\nDue to hazardous road conditions and flooding, NC-12 remains \nclosed from the Marc Basnight Bridge to Rodanthe. The next high \ntides are around 5 PM today. A Coastal Flood Warning remains in\neffect for Hatteras Island and a Coastal Flood Advisory for the\nNorthern Outer Banks. Additionally, a high surf advisory \ncontinues for the beaches from Duck to Cape Hatteras where surf \nheights 8-12 ft will through early evening. Beyond tonight,\naafternooncontinued lowering trend in waves and swell will \ngradually give us a lull Friday before conditions pick up again \n(see key message 3 below). It should be noted that although \nwater levels may fall below typical values that would cause \nflooding, due to the extensive erosion and dune loss, impacts \nFriday may still continue. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Pleasantly cooler air mass will remain with us\nthrough the next several days with temps a bit below climo. \nMaximum temperatures through the period are expected to remain \nin the mid 60s to lower 70s range. Overnight lows will range \nfrom the 40s inland to the 50s coast. Good radiational cooling\nconditions are expected again tonight. With afternoon dewpoints\nin the upper 30s, lows in our typical cold spots should drop\ninto the 30s once again. Model soundings do show just a few more\nknots of wind in the boundary layer compared to yesterday, so we\nmay not be quite as cold as last night.  A bit more mixing \nensues Friday night as strong high pressure builds in from the \nnorth preventing temps from cratering. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Strong high pressure building in from the north\nand low pressure well off the SE coast will result in a period\nof breezy to strong NE winds returning late Friday through the\nweekend with strongest winds along the coast, especially south\nof Cape Hatteras where gradients will be tightest. This, \ncombined with developing King Tides could lead to additional \nminor coastal impacts (coastal flooding and rough surf).",
